import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,ag as r}from"./chunks/framework.CZxGLIO_.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Registration and account security","description":"Understand quotas, trusted proxies, MFA, recovery codes and administrator recovery.","frontmatter":{"title":"Registration and account security","description":"Understand quotas, trusted proxies, MFA, recovery codes and administrator recovery."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/docs/gooseforum/security/index.md","filePath":"en/docs/gooseforum/security/index.md"}'),s={name:"en/docs/gooseforum/security/index.md"};function n(i,e,d,c,l,h){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="registration-and-account-security" tabindex="-1">Registration and account security <a class="header-anchor" href="#registration-and-account-security" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Registration and account security&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Registration counters, authentication caches and MFA challenges include process-local state. Use a <strong>single instance</strong>. Connecting multiple processes to the same database does not make those states shared.</p><h2 id="registration-and-email-quotas" tabindex="-1">Registration and email quotas <a class="header-anchor" href="#registration-and-email-quotas" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Registration and email quotas&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Settings → Security allows hourly registration-request limits. Defaults are:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Registration requests</th><th>Shared password-reset and verification-email quota</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Per trusted IP</td><td>5</td><td>20</td></tr><tr><td>Per target email</td><td>3</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Site-wide</td><td>100</td><td>200</td></tr></tbody></table><p>For configurable registration limits, <code>0</code> disables that dimension. Counters measure requests that pass policy and credential checks, not only successfully created accounts. Each in-memory window lasts one hour from its first request; restarting clears it. Counters are not shared across instances. Verification resends also have account limits of once per minute and three times per day.</p><p>Allowed email domains use exact matching: <code>example.com</code> does not allow <code>sub.example.com</code>. Accounts created with verification requirements retain those requirements; switching to OAuth does not bypass them.</p><h2 id="client-ips-behind-a-proxy" tabindex="-1">Client IPs behind a proxy <a class="header-anchor" href="#client-ips-behind-a-proxy" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Client IPs behind a proxy&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Only proxy IPs or CIDRs in <code>server.trustedProxies</code> are trusted. For an IPv4 reverse proxy on the same host, add this to the existing <code>[server]</code> section:</p><div class="language-toml vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">toml</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">trustedProxies = [</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;127.0.0.1&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">]</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Without this configuration, forwarded headers are not trusted and requests may share the proxy&#39;s IP for rate limiting. Do not trust every address. For containers or separate proxies, list the actual controlled proxy addresses and restrict direct backend access. See <a href="./../deployment/">deployment</a>.</p><h2 id="enable-two-factor-authentication" tabindex="-1">Enable two-factor authentication <a class="header-anchor" href="#enable-two-factor-authentication" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Enable two-factor authentication&quot;">​</a></h2><p>GooseForum supports standard TOTP codes (six digits, 30 seconds) and ten single-use recovery codes. Enabled MFA applies to both password and OAuth login.</p><ol><li>In account settings, enter the local account password to begin binding an authenticator.</li><li>Scan the QR code or enter its secret manually, then confirm with a code. Binding is not active before confirmation.</li><li>Save the recovery codes, which are displayed once and can be downloaded.</li><li>Sign in again to verify. Enabling, disabling or regenerating recovery codes revokes logins on all devices.</li></ol><p>OAuth users must first bind and verify an email address, then set a local password through the password-reset flow. Resetting a password does not disable MFA. Each recovery code works once; generating a new set invalidates all old codes.</p><h2 id="system-secret-and-time" tabindex="-1">System secret and time <a class="header-anchor" href="#system-secret-and-time" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;System secret and time&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>[app].secretKey</code> is used to derive the MFA encryption key. Keep its generated random value and back it up with the database. If the new field is absent, old <code>signingKey</code> configurations are supported; retain the exact value when renaming the field.</p><p>Do not replace the secret with a memorable password or regenerate it after users have enrolled. A lost or malformed key causes verification to fail; generating another configuration cannot decrypt existing data. Keep both server and authenticator clocks synchronized.</p><p>Login challenges expire in five minutes and pending enrollment in ten minutes. Restarting the server requires a new login or enrollment attempt. TOTP codes cannot be reused.</p><h2 id="recover-a-lost-authenticator" tabindex="-1">Recover a lost authenticator <a class="header-anchor" href="#recover-a-lost-authenticator" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Recover a lost authenticator&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Use an unused recovery code first. If all second factors are lost, a full administrator can verify identity and reset MFA under Users → Edit → Account settings, providing a reason and confirmation. The operation revokes sessions and writes an audit record. A limited user administrator cannot do this, and an administrator cannot use this entry point to reset their own MFA.</p><p>There is currently no MFA-reset CLI or email-only bypass. Preserve the system secret and recovery codes; changing a password is not an MFA recovery procedure.</p><hr><p>Sources checked on 2026-11-15：<a href="https://github.com/leancodebox/GooseForum/blob/3044207dea0a9fd5c878406eb0aa094f5798f0ff/docs/registration-controls.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Registration controls</a> · <a href="https://github.com/leancodebox/GooseForum/blob/3044207dea0a9fd5c878406eb0aa094f5798f0ff/docs/two-factor-authentication.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Two-factor authentication</a>。</p>',24)])])}const m=t(s,[["render",n]]);export{p as __pageData,m as default};
